ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1171 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1172,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1173,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1174,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1175,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_26,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_586 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "A Lighter Approach"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "CREATING A DHAMMA COMMUNITY"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "07715dd1fd01a96254e009263f3c7ce5"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 1 ;
    neo:text "Before I began meditating, I was the black sheep of the family. My words carried little to no weight because I was known to lie about things. But about three years into my practice, I spoke less and less, as I watched my speech very carefully. By then, everyone would listen whenever I spoke because what I said was something of value. They listened because they knew that what I said had a lot of thought behind it. Sometimes my family would be unnerved because they knew what I was saying to be the truth."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1176 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1122,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1177,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1178,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1179,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1180,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1181,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_252,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_64,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_947 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "A Lighter Approach"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "CREATING A DHAMMA COMMUNITY"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "ffe4fd75a8e0f597559ca67710b8156c"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 2 ;
    neo:text "My family had regular Sunday social get-togethers where we would eat and then talk. The conversations usually revolved around the latest cars, movie stars, and such. I had been meditating for some time by then and found this to be a waste of time. I considered what could be done and slowly an idea formed in my mind. One day I suggested that we all meditate together. All of us had meditation experience because my father used to bring us all to the monastery. So they agreed to meditate. We all sat for 15 minutes initially and then we would eat."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1182 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1183,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1184,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1185,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1186,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1187,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_190,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_64 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "A Lighter Approach"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "CREATING A DHAMMA COMMUNITY"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "16adcea7ac57ee7b18cf6411e8b1ce58"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 3 ;
    neo:text "After a while the idea crystallized, and we sat together in meditation every time we met. After eating or during the meal I then suggested a discussion and we would talk about life, whether we were happy or unhappy. I would ask them one by one, \"How do you feel about your life? Do you feel satisfied with your life?\" The party would turn serious; one brother replied that he was not satisfied with his life and another replied that it was 50:50. Slowly my siblings began to share the details of their lives and feelings, and someone would cry after sharing and get some relief. It was not something they had considered. Everyone was just sort of going through life, time was passing by, and they'd never given any thought to how they were living their own lives."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1188 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1189,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1190,
        ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1191 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "A Lighter Approach"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "CREATING A DHAMMA COMMUNITY"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "e2df2cd170ee7d48da50f1d8a491f720"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 4 ;
    neo:text "From one week to the next, the questions would continue: If you're not happy with your life, how do you make yourself happy? How do you deal with it? We brought the Dhamma into the conversation and it turned into Dhamma discussions."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_037c2398_17b8_4f53_9503_29faec04f111_1192 a neo:Document ;
